# Doc

`Doc` is HQL's type for a document. Every document is a place, a header and a
body:

```hql
abstract type Doc {
    name   : String
    path   : String
    format : String
    header : Data
    body   : Content
}
```

The body is what a person wrote. The header is everything the document says
about itself. `name`, `path` and `format` are neither: they are facts about the
file, established by the host from the file itself, and they are fields so that
nothing written inside the document can contradict them. An author who writes
`name:` into a metadata block has written an ordinary authored entry about
something else — `header.name` — and the document's own name is untouched. All
of this exists for every document, including a plain Markdown file that carries
no metadata of its own — see [[card-type]] for the narrowed kind of document
that participates in knowledge.

Every field is required and every field is readable. HQL has no field
visibility — values are data and declared fields are queryable data — so nothing
here is hidden from a holder of a document, and no subtype can hide it later.
What a type may say instead is that a field *may be absent*; see [[fields]].

The header is not one of those. Every document has one, empty or not. Where a
format writes authored entries into it, HMD's `metadata` block being the case
that matters, *that* block is optional:

```hql
type HmdHeader {
    metadata? : Data
}
```

A card builds a knowledge layer of its own from those entries and several other
sources — see [[card-type]]. It is a separate field, not this one renamed, and a
document that is not a card has no such layer.

The host adapter described in [[hmd-integration]] supplies both halves from the
existing HMD parser and resolver. The bootstrap evaluator does not yet run any
of this.

## The header exists whether or not it was written

A document's header is a [[data-type|Data]] tree. It is never absent and never
optional: a file with no metadata block has an empty header, not a missing one.
There is no `Data?` to unwrap before every lookup, and no difference in kind
between a document authored without metadata and one whose metadata block was
empty. A missing *key* is still a missing key — that is a question for path
traversal, not for the header.

What a document that exists at all has, whether or not anyone typed it, is
derived by the host — and derived facts about the file are fields, not entries:

| Field | Derivation |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | The file's base name, which is also the slug a wikilink uses |
| `path` | The document's location relative to the vault root |
| `format` | Which document dialect the body is written in |

Where a document was actually read from is a fact of the same kind and belongs
with them; no field declares it yet.

The one derived thing that *is* a header entry is `header.title`, because a
title is the one derived value an author may legitimately contradict. A document
can state a title in its metadata and open with a different first heading, and
the authored entry wins: a heading is prose, and a person who wrote `title:`
meant it. A document with neither is titled by its `name`, so `header.title` is
populated for every document and reading it needs no fallback. The heading a
title displaces stays in the body, where it was written; nothing is lost and
nothing is stored twice.

`name`, `path` and `format` cannot be contradicted this way, because there is
nowhere to write a contradiction: the keys of that name in a header are ordinary
authored entries and no reader of `doc.name` consults them.

## What the header holds, and what it does not

The header is what the document says about itself: the entries a person wrote
into its metadata block, plus `title` when nobody wrote one. Nothing else is
merged into it.

Two other kinds of knowledge about a document stay out, each for its own reason:

- **Derived facts about the file** — `name`, `path`, `format` — are fields.
  Keeping them out of the tree is what makes them incontestable.
- **Contributions from an [[hql/extensions|extension]]** are assembled into
  `card.metadata`, under a key named after the extension, and never into the
  header. A contribution therefore cannot overwrite an authored entry: the two
  are not in the same tree. See [[card-type]].

The rule that falls out of this is that an entry's origin is legible from where
it sits, rather than from provenance attached to a merged value. Provenance
still records what wrote an entry; it does not make the entry true, and a
mechanical entry is not more authoritative than an authored one merely by being
mechanical.

## format

`format` names the dialect the body is written in.

```hql
doc.format   // md | hmd
```

This matters because the document features HQL can rely on are not universal.
Plain Markdown has no wikilinks, no embeds and no namespace. HyperMarkDown adds
the hyper layer on top of an unchanged CommonMark base, which is why every `.md`
file is already valid `.hmd` — the relation between the two formats is inclusion,
not translation.

`format` is therefore the entry that says which of the operations below apply.
Asking a `md` document for its uplinks is a well-formed question with an empty
answer; asking it to resolve `[[alice]]` is not a question at all, because the
text is not a link in that dialect.

The authored block belongs on this side of the line. A YAML block fenced by
`---` — *frontmatter* in Markdown's and HMD's vocabulary — is a format
convention, not the definition of a header. It is one format-specific way of
supplying the authored layer, and a format that supplies metadata some other way
— or not at all — still has a header. That is why the field is `header`, and why
HMD's term for one way of writing it is not used as a type name anywhere in
HQL.

Which formats HQL admits beyond `md` and `hmd`, and whether `format` is a closed
enumeration or an open name that extensions can register, are open. So is
whether a document can be reinterpreted under a different format.

## Body structure

| Type | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `Content` | Parsed body content, whatever the dialect |
| `HmdContent` | Parsed HyperMarkDown content; `HmdContent : Content` |
| `Section` | A heading and the content beneath it |
| `Block` | Anchored content addressable by name |
| `Link` | A reference from this document to a target |

A section runs from its heading to the next heading of equal or higher level —
the same rule HMD uses when expanding a heading fragment. Sections nest, so a
document's headings form a tree rather than a flat list. Whether `Doc` exposes
that tree, a flat sequence, or both is open.

```hql
doc.body      : Content
doc.sections  : Seq<Section>
```

`Hmd` is not a type here. It names a *format* — the dialect `doc.format`
reports — and a format is not the parsed value that results from reading a
document in it. The parsed value is `HmdContent`, and `Content` is the type a
document's body has before anything is known about which dialect produced it.
Their relation is the usual narrowing: a body is `Content`, and establishing
`doc.format` is `hmd` is what establishes it as `HmdContent`, with the
addressing and embed operations below attached to that stronger type.

Keeping content apart from `Doc` is also what lets an embedded fragment or a
constructed body be a value without inventing a header for it.

Whether `Content` is one type with narrowings per dialect, or a union, and what
a body is before it has been parsed at all, are open.

## Addressing

HyperMarkDown addresses content at three granularities, and HQL should be able
to name all three:

```hql
[[alice]]           // the document
[[alice#Bio]]       // a heading fragment
[[alice#^claim]]    // an anchored block
```

A bracket pair is a doc reference, defined in [[link-op]] along with the `->`
edge form built from it. What matters here is what it addresses: a reference to a
part carries the type of that part, so the three granularities above are `Doc`,
`Section` and `Block`.

```hql
bio : Section = [[alice#Bio]]
```

What resolution does **not** hand back is a [[card-type|Card]]. `[[alice]]` names
a document, and card-ness is a knowledge property that has to be established
rather than assumed: a reference cannot know that its target represents a piece
of knowledge, and only a checked narrowing can say so.

```hql
doc  : Doc  = [[alice]]
card : Card = doc as Card        // a narrowing, and it can fail
```

The spelling of that step is unresolved, and so is what it produces when the
document turns out not to be a card. Resolution can fail too, in HMD's own ways:
a target that does not exist, a fragment missing from a document that does, a
name matching several documents, or a target escaping the namespace root. HMD treats an unresolved link as a warning, deliberately —
writing forward to a card that has not been written yet is permitted, and the
diagnostic report is a to-do list. HQL must not turn that into a hard error by
accident. How failure is carried remains open.

Resolution needs a namespace, which a bare document does not have. A bare name
is resolved by named imports, then the spine walk outward from the document's own
directory, then wildcard import origins, then autodiscovery — and ambiguity is an
error with no ranking rule. That machinery belongs to the vault, so it belongs to
[[card-type]]. HQL consumes the outcome, and must preserve unresolved and
ambiguous references as outcomes rather than silently picking a document.

## Links

A link is an edge from this document to a reference, and an authored `[[bob]]`
is `[[this]] -> [[bob]]` with whatever data the author attached — see
[[link-op]]. Display text declares nothing: `[[alice|Alice B.]]` links the same
way `[[alice]]` does, and data on an edge does not assert a relationship type.

Links traverse in two directions. `uplinks` are the document's outgoing
references; `downlinks` are the references that point at it. A document can be
its own uplink and downlink. The older `backlinks` spelling is dropped because it
named only one half of the relation.

Downlinks are the direction that needs a vault: a document cannot know what
points at it by reading itself. Uplinks are readable from the body alone.

When a relationship needs identity, provenance, discussion or its own incoming
links, it should stop being a link and become a `RelationCard` — see
[[hql/knowledge]]. That is the boundary this card is defending: a link stays
cheap and anonymous, and meaning lives in knowledge.

## Embeds

An embed pulls another document, heading or block into this one:

```text
![[glossary/token#^definition]]
```

Embeds raise a question `Doc` has to answer explicitly: does a document's content
include what it transcluded? Both readings are useful. Rendering erases the
boundary, and the reader sees one continuous page. Analysis usually must not: a
word count, a link inventory or an evidence check should distinguish what this
document asserts from what it borrowed.

The current direction is that `Doc` preserves the boundary and expansion is a
separate operation, so that provenance survives. HMD's own limits apply to any
expansion — cycles are an error and nesting is bounded — and an embedded block
keeps its defining document as its origin.

## Doc is not text

Reducing a document to its source string would make HQL a text-search language
over `.hmd` files, which [[hql/core]] explicitly rejects. `Doc` is structure: a
pipeline selects sections, follows links and inspects anchors, and only reaches
for raw text where text is genuinely what is wanted.

The same applies in the other direction. Constructing a `Doc` produces a value;
it does not write a file. Persistence is an explicit effect, and rendering
belongs to the host — see the presenter vocabulary in [[hql/knowledge]].

Serialization back to a file is unresolved, and is not merely the inverse of
parsing: a round trip has to decide what happens to formatting the parser did
not keep, whether expanded embeds collapse back into their embed syntax, and
which header layers are written out at all. A derived `path` must not be
serialized into an authored metadata block.

See [[card-type]] for documents that participate in knowledge, [[data-type]] for
the header's tree type, [[pipes]] for composition and [[hql/design-status]] for
open decisions.
